      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Acute stress caused by strong emotions such as fear can sometimes cause sudden death in people with underlying coronary artery disease (CAD). Chronic mental stress may also promote the long-term development of coronary disease, although the distinction between Type A and Type B personalities appears overly simplistic. Stress-management interventions measurably improve CAD patients' performance on cardiac function tests, and should be incorporated more often in standard cardiac rehabilitation programs."},
